From a63d2a3105e03ce9f774f8f6d13c8d29bee340cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JerryNee <ni12@illinois.edu>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 17:21:10 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] Add -p2p: advertise and accept AirPlay over Apple
 peer-to-peer

A receiver published only on the infrastructure interfaces depends on the
network it sits on. Managed networks filter or proxy mDNS, and many isolate
clients from each other, so UxPlay is invisible or unreachable on exactly the
networks where a shared display is most useful -- the recurring complaint in
issues #234 and #163. Publishing over AWDL as well lets a client reach UxPlay
directly, needing neither a shared network nor cooperation from its operator.

Two things are required, and neither works alone:

- Register with kDNSServiceFlagsIncludeP2P | kDNSServiceFlagsIncludeAWDL on
  kDNSServiceInterfaceIndexAny. Pinning the registration to awdl0 instead
  hides the service from clients using other peer-to-peer interfaces, which
  is what makes IncludeP2P look ineffective when tried on its own.

- Set SO_RECV_ANYIF on the listening sockets. A conventional BSD listener
  silently drops packets delivered over AWDL, so without it the receiver
  appears in the client's list but the server never sees the connection --
  no bytes on the port, nothing logged, just a client that cannot connect.

Both are off unless -p2p is given, so default behaviour is unchanged: without
the option the service still resolves to the host's own name, with it to the
randomized UUID name mDNSResponder publishes for peer-to-peer registrations.

SO_RECV_ANYIF is not in the public SDK headers; the value is taken from XNU's
private socket.h. That is the reason this is opt-in and macOS-only. Note the
scope: this makes UxPlay findable and reachable over an AWDL link. It does
not implement Apple's peer-to-peer AirPlay handshake -- the media session is
the ordinary one, over that link instead of the infrastructure network.

Also flush the log line as it is written, so output stays readable when
stdout is a pipe rather than a terminal.

